1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,350
A mango.

2
00:00:01,241 --> 00:00:02,618
A grain of wheat.

3
00:00:02,618 --> 00:00:03,564
A housefly.

4
00:00:03,564 --> 00:00:04,464
And you.

5
00:00:04,755 --> 00:00:06,090
Four living things.

6
00:00:06,090 --> 00:00:09,969
In biology, each one has a
full address, seven lines long.

7
00:00:10,419 --> 00:00:14,883
Diya saw a housefly land on a mango in
her kitchen, right next to the wheat

8
00:00:14,883 --> 00:00:15,783
flour.

9
00:00:15,443 --> 00:00:18,787
She asked: how close are
these living things, really?

10
00:00:19,137 --> 00:00:24,090
Today we walk each one up the ladder of
ranks, step by step, and find exactly

11
00:00:24,090 --> 00:00:25,598
where they part ways.

12
00:00:26,248 --> 00:00:27,847
First, the ladder itself.

13
00:00:28,197 --> 00:00:29,942
There are seven main ranks.

14
00:00:29,942 --> 00:00:33,827
Species, genus, family, order,
class, phylum, and kingdom.

15
00:00:34,277 --> 00:00:36,360
A rank is one level of grouping.

16
00:00:36,360 --> 00:00:39,618
Each group sits inside a
bigger group, one level up.

17
00:00:40,068 --> 00:00:43,478
Going up, each group holds
more kinds of living things.

18
00:00:43,478 --> 00:00:45,746
But those kinds share fewer features.

19
00:00:46,396 --> 00:00:48,062
Let's climb with the mango.

20
00:00:48,412 --> 00:00:50,055
Look at the plant first.

21
00:00:50,055 --> 00:00:51,998
Its leaf has a net of veins.

22
00:00:51,998 --> 00:00:56,308
Its seed sits inside a fruit, and
the seed has two seed leaves, called

23
00:00:56,308 --> 00:00:57,208
cotyledons.

24
00:00:57,616 --> 00:00:59,155
Step one, species.

25
00:00:59,155 --> 00:01:01,362
The mango is Mangifera indica.

26
00:01:01,362 --> 00:01:05,960
A species is one kind of organism,
with the same basic features.

27
00:01:06,460 --> 00:01:07,963
Step two, genus.

28
00:01:07,963 --> 00:01:08,878
Mangifera.

29
00:01:08,878 --> 00:01:12,370
A genus is a group of
closely related species.

30
00:01:12,870 --> 00:01:14,423
Step three, family.

31
00:01:14,423 --> 00:01:15,483
Anacardiaceae.

32
00:01:15,483 --> 00:01:18,243
A family is a group of related genera.

33
00:01:18,243 --> 00:01:21,004
The cashew tree is in this family too.

34
00:01:21,504 --> 00:01:23,018
Step four, order.

35
00:01:23,018 --> 00:01:23,962
Sapindales.

36
00:01:23,962 --> 00:01:26,957
An order is a group of related families.

37
00:01:27,457 --> 00:01:28,799
Step five, class.

38
00:01:28,799 --> 00:01:32,533
Dicotyledonae, the plants with
two cotyledons in each seed.

39
00:01:33,033 --> 00:01:33,933
Step six.

40
00:01:33,836 --> 00:01:37,713
For plants, this rank is called
a division, not a phylum.

41
00:01:37,713 --> 00:01:42,482
The mango is in Angiospermae, the
flowering plants with seeds inside fruits.

42
00:01:42,982 --> 00:01:44,360
Step seven, kingdom.

43
00:01:44,360 --> 00:01:45,260
Plantae.

44
00:01:44,999 --> 00:01:47,693
Every plant on Earth
sits in this one group.

45
00:01:48,343 --> 00:01:49,651
Now the housefly.

46
00:01:49,651 --> 00:01:51,808
Its body tells us its address.

47
00:01:52,158 --> 00:01:55,027
It has a head, a thorax and an abdomen.

48
00:01:55,027 --> 00:01:58,310
Three body parts and six
legs make it an insect.

49
00:01:58,310 --> 00:01:59,391
Class Insecta.

50
00:01:59,891 --> 00:02:01,570
Its legs are jointed.

51
00:02:01,570 --> 00:02:05,312
Animals with jointed legs
belong to phylum Arthropoda.

52
00:02:05,812 --> 00:02:07,963
It has only one pair of wings.

53
00:02:07,963 --> 00:02:10,510
Di means two, and ptera means wings.

54
00:02:10,510 --> 00:02:12,245
So its order is Diptera.

55
00:02:12,745 --> 00:02:14,566
Its family is Muscidae.

56
00:02:14,566 --> 00:02:18,676
Its genus is Musca, and its
species is Musca domestica.

57
00:02:18,676 --> 00:02:20,553
Its kingdom is Animalia.

58
00:02:21,203 --> 00:02:23,248
Now put the mango beside wheat.

59
00:02:23,598 --> 00:02:24,498
Mango.

60
00:02:24,240 --> 00:02:28,476
Two cotyledons in the seed,
and leaves with a net of veins.

61
00:02:28,476 --> 00:02:29,919
Class Dicotyledonae.

62
00:02:30,369 --> 00:02:31,269
Wheat.

63
00:02:30,993 --> 00:02:35,296
One cotyledon in the grain, and
long leaves with parallel veins.

64
00:02:35,296 --> 00:02:36,799
Class Monocotyledonae.

65
00:02:36,799 --> 00:02:41,000
Its order is Poales, and its
family is Poaceae, the grasses.

66
00:02:41,450 --> 00:02:45,443
But both make flowers, and both
keep their seeds inside fruits.

67
00:02:45,443 --> 00:02:49,475
So both share the division
Angiospermae, and the kingdom Plantae.

68
00:02:50,125 --> 00:02:51,025
Your turn.

69
00:02:50,989 --> 00:02:52,211
Mango and wheat.

70
00:02:52,211 --> 00:02:54,922
At which rank do they first split apart?

71
00:02:54,922 --> 00:02:56,193
Pause and decide.

72
00:02:59,393 --> 00:03:00,293
Class!

73
00:02:59,973 --> 00:03:02,512
They share the kingdom and the division.

74
00:03:02,512 --> 00:03:05,801
But the mango is a dicot,
and wheat is a monocot.

75
00:03:06,451 --> 00:03:07,379
Remember this.

76
00:03:07,379 --> 00:03:11,490
The higher the rank, the more kinds
it holds, and the fewer features they

77
00:03:11,490 --> 00:03:12,390
share.

78
00:03:12,620 --> 00:03:14,146
Now, a worked example.

79
00:03:14,146 --> 00:03:17,733
Let's write the full address
of a human, from the top down.

80
00:03:18,083 --> 00:03:19,530
Kingdom Animalia.

81
00:03:19,530 --> 00:03:20,929
We are animals.

82
00:03:21,379 --> 00:03:22,542
Phylum Chordata.

83
00:03:22,542 --> 00:03:27,312
Early in life, as an embryo, we have
a notochord, a stiff rod along the

84
00:03:27,312 --> 00:03:28,212
back.

85
00:03:28,319 --> 00:03:29,388
Class Mammalia.

86
00:03:29,388 --> 00:03:32,578
We have hair, and mothers
feed their babies milk.

87
00:03:33,028 --> 00:03:36,388
Order Primata, the group
with monkeys and gorillas.

88
00:03:36,388 --> 00:03:37,601
Family Hominidae.

89
00:03:38,051 --> 00:03:40,773
Genus Homo, and species Homo sapiens.

90
00:03:40,773 --> 00:03:43,771
Seven lines, and the address is complete.

91
00:03:44,421 --> 00:03:45,886
Now, the exam trap.

92
00:03:46,236 --> 00:03:49,849
Many students say Diptera is
the family of the housefly.

93
00:03:50,249 --> 00:03:52,065
But Diptera is its order.

94
00:03:52,065 --> 00:03:53,720
Its family is Muscidae.

95
00:03:53,720 --> 00:03:57,463
Animal family names end
in the letters I, D, A, E.

96
00:03:57,463 --> 00:04:00,741
Plant family names end in A, C, E, A, E.

97
00:04:01,391 --> 00:04:02,977
Where is this asked?

98
00:04:02,977 --> 00:04:07,129
NEET has asked which organism
belongs to the family Muscidae.

99
00:04:07,129 --> 00:04:10,951
Expect match the rank questions
on these four organisms.

100
00:04:11,551 --> 00:04:12,760
Exam level now.

101
00:04:12,760 --> 00:04:14,287
Read the four pairs.

102
00:04:14,287 --> 00:04:16,542
Which ones are correctly matched?

103
00:04:16,542 --> 00:04:17,803
Pause and solve.

104
00:04:23,203 --> 00:04:24,405
The answer is C.

105
00:04:24,405 --> 00:04:26,223
Pairs one, three and four.

106
00:04:26,723 --> 00:04:27,790
Two is wrong.

107
00:04:27,790 --> 00:04:29,826
Poales is the order of wheat.

108
00:04:29,826 --> 00:04:31,397
Its family is Poaceae.

109
00:04:31,897 --> 00:04:34,358
One, three and four match the table.

110
00:04:34,358 --> 00:04:36,598
Diptera, Mammalia and Angiospermae.

111
00:04:37,198 --> 00:04:38,657
Here is your recap.

112
00:04:38,657 --> 00:04:40,157
Screenshot this card.

113
00:04:43,357 --> 00:04:46,193
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

114
00:04:46,593 --> 00:04:47,493
One.

115
00:04:47,076 --> 00:04:50,330
Which class holds wheat,
and which holds the mango?

116
00:04:51,230 --> 00:04:52,130
Two.

117
00:04:51,727 --> 00:04:54,440
Why is the housefly in the order Diptera?

118
00:04:55,340 --> 00:04:56,240
Three.

119
00:04:55,941 --> 00:04:59,400
Which is the only rank a
human and a housefly share?

120
00:05:00,500 --> 00:05:04,712
Next time: the same fish has a
different name in every language.

121
00:05:04,712 --> 00:05:06,892
So which name do scientists use?

122
00:05:06,892 --> 00:05:09,319
We find out in binomial nomenclature.
